    "number": 276,
    "title": "fix: redundant_env_clone lint misses UFCS-style Env::clone(&env) calls",
    "body": " Closes #76 \r\n    \r\n    ### Summary\r\n    This PR fixes a bug where the `redundant_env_clone` lint would completely miss `.clone()` calls on an `Env` receiver if they were written\r\n  using fully-qualified/UFCS syntax (e.g., `Env::clone(&env)` or `Clone::clone(&env)`).\r\n    \r\n    ### What Changed\r\n    - Extended `RedundantEnvClone::check_expr` in `soroban_cost_lints/src/lib.rs` to match `hir::ExprKind::Call` alongside the existing\r\n  `hir::ExprKind::MethodCall`. \r\n    - Used the `match_soroban_def_path` helper to detect paths ending in `Env::clone` or `Clone::clone`.\r\n    - Added UI test fixtures for both UFCS variants, including their corresponding `#[allow(...)]` checks, and manually updated the `.stderr`\r\n  snapshots.\r\n    \r\n    ### Key Design Decisions\r\n    To identify UFCS clone calls, I utilized the existing suffix-based string matching (`match_soroban_def_path(..., &[\"Clone\", \"clone\"])`)\r\n  rather than doing a strict DefId trait lookup. This perfectly matches the established codebase conventions and reliably captures both\r\n  `core::clone::Clone` and `std::clone::Clone`. \r\n    \r\n    ### Acceptance Criteria\r\n    - [x] `.clone()` on an Env receiver is detected regardless of whether it's written as a method call or in fully-qualified/UFCS form.\r\n    - [x] A new fixture case using `Env::clone(&env)` (or `Clone::clone(&env)`) is confirmed to trigger the lint.\r\n    - [x] The existing method-call-syntax detection and the `#[allow(...)]` suppression mechanism continue to pass unchanged.\r\n    \r\n    ### Test Output & Coverage\r\n    - Tests pass locally. \r\n    - Lint UI tests capture the two new UFCS instances identically to the method-call variants.\r\n    - *(Note: as this is a compiler lint plugin checked via AST snapshot tests, standard code coverage thresholds are not applicable).*\r\n    \r\n    ### Security Note\r\n    There are no new dependencies, no unsafe code introduced, and no client-side trust assumptions modified. The lint purely statically\r\n  analyzes the guest AST before compilation.",
